# Constraint propagation through higher-order function arguments.
# Verifies that a constraint-bearing function type flows correctly when passed
# as an argument to a higher-order function (HOF).
#
# The HOF `apply-fn` has no explicit type annotation — its parameter types are
# inferred. When called with a constrained lambda (a -> String | Describable a)
# and an Int, the type checker must:
#   1. Unify the HOF's first-param type with the constrained function type.
#   2. Allow the constrained lambda to be applied to 42 (Int satisfies Describable).
#
# At the eval level the constraint is erased: the result is always String("42").
# This test documents the ACTUAL behavior: eval succeeds regardless of whether
# the constraint propagates fully through the HOF's inferred type, because
# constraint checking happens at the call site of the constrained lambda, not
# inside the HOF body.
[
  # User-defined class and Int instance
  Describable: [class [let Describable a]]
  DescribableInt: [instance Describable [pattern [Int]]:]

  # Higher-order function: takes a function f and a value p, applies f to p.
  # No explicit type annotation — types are fully inferred.
  apply-fn: [fn [f p] [f p]]

  # Call apply-fn with a constrained lambda and an Int argument.
  # The lambda converts a Describable value to its String representation.
  result: [apply-fn [fn@[bind: [a]  constraint: [a: Describable]  return: String] [x@a] [str x]] 42]
]

---

%.result
=== out
String("42")
